  "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e84{e}^{-4t},\\,5{e}^{5t}\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int 4{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 5{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-{e}^{-4t}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{5t}+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=\u27e81,6\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\u27e8-{e}^{-4(0)}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{5(0)}+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e81,6\u27e9 & =\u27e8-1+{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=2$ and ${C}_{2}=5.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=\u27e8-{e}^{-4t}+2,\\,{e}^{5t}+5\u27e9.\n$$",
